pd.get_dummies() in Pandas

pd.get_dummies() is used to convert categorical/text data into numbers.

This is called One-Hot Encoding.

Why do we need it?

Machine Learning models generally work with numbers, not text.

Suppose we have:
----------------

import pandas as pd

df = pd.DataFrame({
    "name": ["Anu", "Ravi", "Kumar"],
    "service": ["Facial", "Haircut", "Facial"]
})

=======

Our service column contains text:

| name  | service |
| ----- | ------- |
| Anu   | Facial  |
| Ravi  | Haircut |
| Kumar | Facial  |


====

A machine-learning model cannot directly use "Facial" and "Haircut" as numerical features.

So:

pd.get_dummies(df["service"])

===

gives:

| Facial | Haircut |
| -----: | ------: |
|   True |   False |
|  False |    True |
|   True |   False |


====

You can also get 0 and 1:

pd.get_dummies(df["service"], dtype=int)

Result:

| Facial | Haircut |
| -----: | ------: |
|      1 |       0 |
|      0 |       1 |
|      1 |       0 |


====
💇 Real-time beauty parlour example

Suppose:

df = pd.DataFrame({
    "customer": ["Anu", "Priya", "Divya", "Kavya"],
    "service": ["Facial", "Haircut", "Manicure", "Facial"]
})

Apply:

df = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=["service"], dtype=int)

Result:

| customer | service_Facial | service_Haircut | service_Manicure |
| -------- | -------------: | --------------: | ---------------: |
| Anu      |              1 |               0 |                0 |
| Priya    |              0 |               1 |                0 |
| Divya    |              0 |               0 |                1 |
| Kavya    |              1 |               0 |                0 |

Now the categorical service has been converted into numerical columns.
===

Text/Categorical
       ↓
pd.get_dummies()
       ↓
0 and 1
       ↓
Machine Learning

So:

pd.get_dummies() = Convert categorical values into separate 0/1 columns (One-Hot Encoding).

=====

